#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056016 :tutorial/content "To **evaluate** the expression $3+x$ for\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\n, we replace $x$ with $\\color{blue}2$ in the expression and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 + x &=  \\\\\n3 + (2) &=  \\\\\n3 + 2 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f4532e0a-1865-5de3-b251-2774b7d5d45d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8079 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056017 :tutorial/content "The usual order of operations (PEMDAS) applies when evaluating expressions. So when an algebraic expression contains parentheses $(\\ldots)$, we calculate what's inside the parentheses *first*.\n\nFor example, we evaluate $6 - (2x+3)$ at $x=3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 - (2x + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - (2 \\cdot 3 + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - (6 + 3) &=  \\\\\n6 - 9 &=  \\\\\n&= - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34dded6d-159e-5c07-9693-340cd7e2080e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8081 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing Parentheses"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056018 :tutorial/content "If an expression contains more than one variable, we evaluate it using the same procedure, replacing each variable with the appropriate value.\n\nFor example, to find the value of\n\n$$\n2b + \\dfrac{1}{2}a\n$$\n\nwhen $a=-2$ and $b=3$, we replace $a$ with $-2$ and $b$ with $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2b + \\frac{1}{2}a &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot 3 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-2) &=  \\\\\n6 - 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5858aaa3-b757-5794-a965-368222b8122f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8082 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Expressions Containing More Than One Variable"}]}
